WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.000
Ah, the great British banger.

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:03.280
Or the lovely salami.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:04.680
Or the battered saveloy.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:08.600
Hang on, just what is a sausage?
And why do they taste so good?

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.320
The concept of sausages
goes back to antiquity -

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.200
stuffing animal guts with a meat,
fish or sweet batter mixture

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.720
was an obvious and practical way

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.320
to make easily cooked,
transportable food.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.360
Originally, they were seen
as small puddings –

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.080
a large pudding was a haggis,

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:24.240
done in a stomach,
rather than a length of gut -

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.600
and, like puddings, they were
often boiled rather than fried.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.600
The French word for sausage, boudin,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:31.880
even gave us British
the word ‘pudding’,

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:33.800
since early puddings were sausages,

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.200
and sausages really were
a type of pudding.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.280
Sometimes they were smoked
or air dried to keep through winter.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.280
Everywhere, though,
for those who could afford it,

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.280
a spiced fresh meat sausage
was a pretty staple item.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.120
Even for those who couldn’t afford
good meat and fine spice,

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.360
sausages were about
from very early on.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.120
There was a play called The Sausage
Seller written in the 5th Century BC,

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.440
and already then the
lower-grade sausage

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:57.480
had a whiff of uncertain meat

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.120
disguised with judicious
sauce about it.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.280
In the medieval era, northern,
wetter climes such as the UK

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.760
developed a speciality
in fresh meat sausages,

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:07.280
to be cooked before eating.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.760
Elsewhere, in dry climates
such as the Mediterranean

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.200
which had good, stiff
breezes and mountains,

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.520
dried or smoked sausages
were more popular.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:15.920
There they would cure properly,

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.880
and not simply go green
as they would in Britain.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.160
There were still mixtures
with fish, fruit and blood –

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.400
black pudding for example –

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.600
but as the centuries rolled on,

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.800
most European sausages
became based on a mixture

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.240
of pork, pork fat and flavourings.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.280
They were not posh,
and by the 18th Century

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.520
they rarely appeared at the
dinner tables of the wealthy -

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.760
though they happily ate them
as suppers and snacks.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.440
But they were very popular,

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.000
and available for almost
every possible budget.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.600
Mass production became possible
by the mid-Victorian times,

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.920
and even cheaper sausages came out,
with a hefty filler of bread or rusk.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.040
Saveloys in particular
gained a reputation

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.280
for being based
on off-cuts and filler,

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:53.520
or indeed, horsemeat and sawdust
in some people’s view.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:55.720
Then, as now, the dirt-cheap versions

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.000
rubbed shoulders with
the more expensive ones –

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.200
until the First World War,

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.800
when meat shortages meant that no-one
got much meat in their casings.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.240
One theory as to why
they became known as bangers,

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:06.320
shortly after the war,

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.440
is that the level of filler meant
they banged and popped when fried.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.640
However, it’s just as likely
to come from their shape,

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.400
which resembled shell casings,

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.040
not least as they were still
often boiled to cook them.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:17.800
Normally, they are cylindrical,

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.280
but they can, on occasion,

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:20.800
be square like a Lorne.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.480
Today, eighty-five percent of Brits
regularly consume sausages,

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.040
plain, as bangers and mash
or as in toad-in-the-hole –

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.960
though globally it’s the cocktail
sausage which is the king,

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.360
for a bite-sized piece
of porky glory.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:35.400
Small or large, contents as you wish,

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:37.440
we’ve had sausages
for thousands of years,

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.480
and they are clearly here to stay.

